WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.300
Function calling, in the agent context.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.933
The happy path is easy.

3
00:00:04.933 --> 00:00:08.467
Production is about what happens when a tool call goes wrong.

4
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.200
Production tool calling has three realities.

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.637
Parallel calls: the model may request several tools in one turn.

6
00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:21.033
Run them concurrently, and return every result with its matching ID.

7
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:27.633
Malformed arguments: hallucinated fields, wrong date formats, ranges the API rejects.

8
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:29.800
And the big one: error feedback.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.167
Here is the rule: write error messages for the model, not for humans.

10
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.033
Error four hundred teaches the model nothing.

11
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:44.533
But a message that states the date format and the seven day limit, and what you sent, lets it fix its own call.

12
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.100
The error message is the repair prompt.

13
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.500
Put it together and the loop heals itself.

14
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.133
The model calls a tool.

15
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:53.433
The call fails.

16
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.400
You return the error as the tool's observation.

17
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.600
And on the next turn, the model repairs its own call.

18
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.367
Here is the pattern in code.

19
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:06.900
First, a pydantic gate validates the arguments before the tool runs.

20
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.300
If validation or the API fails, we build a clear message that says how to fix it.

21
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.556
Either way, the text goes back into the messages.

22
00:01:15.556 --> 00:01:16.400
Never a crash.

23
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.100
Here is a real example.

24
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.867
Asked for close approaches this month, the agent requests a thirty day span.

25
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:27.996
NASA's API rejects it, because the feed caps at seven days.

26
00:01:27.996 --> 00:01:30.333
Your error string states that limit.

27
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:35.556
So the next turn splits the month into four weekly calls and merges the results.

28
00:01:35.556 --> 00:01:38.633
One good message turned a crash into a capability.

29
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:40.672
Let's recap.

30
00:01:40.672 --> 00:01:44.867
Handle parallel calls, and return results with matching IDs.

31
00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.967
Expect malformed arguments, and validate them.

32
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:51.267
Return errors as observations, never crashes.

33
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:53.851
And write errors the model can act on.

34
00:01:53.851 --> 00:01:54.933
Next up: planning.
